Falsifies `paging::nth_allocation::a_failed_map_block_leaks_no_table`.

The defect: an "atomic" `map_block`. On `OutOfPageFrames` it clears the first table entry this
call installed, so nothing it built stays reachable. That reads as cleanup and is a leak: the mapper
does not own frames and cannot free them, so the tables below the cleared entry are handed out and
reachable from nothing, and teardown (which frees what the tables reach) never sees them. It is the
most likely shape of a future "make a failed map leave no trace" change.

Expected to fail: assertion (1), "table frame(s) handed out and unreachable from the root", first at
`map_block(Size2MiB)` with allocation 1 failed on aarch64 (the table at level 1 is orphaned).

`script/falsifications --sweep paging` replays it, weekly and per pull request, since milestone
742 (every test is falsified as routine). By hand, the same thing is:

    git apply crates/paging/falsifications/nth_allocation.a_failed_map_block_leaks_no_table.patch
    cargo test -p paging --test nth_allocation a_failed_map_block_leaks_no_table        # must fail
    git apply -R crates/paging/falsifications/nth_allocation.a_failed_map_block_leaks_no_table.patch

Run on 2026-10-04 (UTC), patagonia: red as predicted, green again after the revert.

